    "durationMs": 337532,
    "finalText": "The URL Shortener API is complete and fully verified — 32/32 functional checks passed, plus 9/9 post-restart persistence checks.\n\n**Implementation** (Express 4.21.2 + `pg`, the idiomatic node-postgres layer for Express+Postgres):\n- `server.js` — all endpoints: `POST /links` (validates absolute http/https URL with host, custom or generated 4–20 char codes, 409 on duplicate, 422 on malformed JSON/invalid url/invalid code), `GET /links/:code`, `GET /r/:code` (302 with atomic `clicks = clicks + 1` via `UPDATE ... RETURNING`, exactly once per request), `GET /links` (ascending creation order with `items`/`total`).\n- `migrate.js` + `migrations/001_create_links.sql` — migration runner tracked in a `schema_migrations` table, so re-runs only apply pending migrations (verified: \"Skipping already applied\" on restart).\n- `start.sh` — applies migrations then serves in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`; no install step. `DATABASE_URL` is read from the environment, nothing hard-coded.\n- `README.md` — includes a \"How to start\" section.\n\n**Verification highlights**: exact 201/409/404/422 behavior with JSON error bodies, redirect `Location` equals the stored URL verbatim, `GET /links` doesn't increment clicks, and links plus click counts survived a full server restart. Test data was cleared afterward and the server stopped, so port 8000 is free and the database is clean for the grader's `sh start.sh` run."
  }
